<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgian Orthodox Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Georgian Orthodoxy, Georgian Apostolic Church</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> is the Eastern Orthodox church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, dating from the conversion of Kartli in the 4th century. It broke with the Armenian church over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in the early 7th century, a split that shaped allegiances in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Medieval Georgian kings spread it to neighbouring lands such as Hereti and to Armenian nobles in their service.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Georgian Orthodox Church" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rastrelli F · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Georgian_Orthodox_Church.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Georgian Orthodox Church is an autocephalous Eastern Orthodox church in full communion with the other Orthodox churches. It asserts an apostolic foundation and traces its roots to the Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and Colchis, in modern eastern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, by the apostle Andrew in the 1st century and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nino">Saint Nino</a> in the 4th. Modern historians consider the Andrew account mythical, the product of a late tradition from 9th-century Byzantine legends. In the 4th and 5th centuries the Church of Iberia was strictly subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, where its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were consecrated, and around 480 the Byzantine government recognised its chief prelate as a catholicos instead of an archbishop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between the 11th and early 13th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> united western and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in one kingdom, relying on the church's prestige and granting it tax immunity and large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>. In 1103 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> the Builder convened the council of Ruisi-Urbnisi, which condemned Armenian Miaphysitism more strongly than before. Georgian monasticism began in the 6th century with the Thirteen Assyrian Fathers, who founded David Gareja, and its golden age lasted from the 9th to the 11th century, with monasteries abroad such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iviron-monastery">Iviron</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>.</p><p>The Mongol invasions of the 13th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a>'s in the 14th and 15th gravely disrupted Georgian Christianity. In the west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicate-of-abkhazia">Catholicate of Abkhazia</a> emerged under the kings of Imereti and lasted until 1795. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed Kartl-Kakheti in 1801, its authorities abolished the church's autocephaly on 18 July 1811 and replaced the Georgian liturgy with Church Slavonic. On 25 January 1990 the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> recognised autocephaly again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The church is governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-synod">Holy Synod</a>, the body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, headed by the Catholicos-Patriarch of All Georgia. The first head of the church to carry the title of patriarch was Melkisedek I, from 1010 to 1033. Eastern Orthodoxy was the state religion for most of Georgia's history until 1921, when Soviet forces occupied the country. The constitution recognises the church's special role in Georgia's history and states its independence from the state, and relations are further regulated by the Concordat of 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georgian Orthodox Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Georgia has about 3.9 million people, 3,914,000 at the census of November 2024, over a third of them in Tbilisi; 86.8 percent are Georgians, and 82 percent belong to the <b>Georgian Orthodox Church</b>, with Muslims 11.1 percent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Georgian Orthodox Church</b> was the largest landowner after the crown, and Georgian monasteries flourished abroad, notably the Iviron on Mount Athos, the Monastery of the Cross in Jerusalem and Petritsoni in Bulgaria, linking the kingdom to the wider Orthodox world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious life centred on the <b>Georgian Orthodox Church</b>, whose western branch was headed by the Catholicos of Abkhazia, and Catholic missionaries also worked in the region in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its speakers seem to have begun writing it when the Kingdom of Iberia was Christianised in the mid-4th century, and the <b>Georgian Orthodox Church</b> kept its oldest scripts in use for its writings.</blockquote><a href="/language/georgian">Georgian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 10th century Queen Dinar, of Georgian Bagratid descent, converted the principality to <b>Georgian Orthodoxy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hereti">Principality of Hereti</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivane converted to <b>Georgian Orthodoxy</b> while Zakare stayed in the Armenian church.</blockquote><a href="/state/zakarid-armenia">Zakarid Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
